---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { Navigation } from '@/components/Navigation';
import { Footer } from '@/components/Footer';
import { ConsentBanner } from '@/components/ConsentBanner';
---

<BaseLayout 
  title="Why Does Pain Keep Coming Back After Treatment? | Scandinavian Clinic, North Vancouver"
  description="Why muscle and joint pain returns after treatment — the root causes that surface treatment misses, what a thorough assessment finds, and how retraining movement and stabilisation helps."
>
  <div class="min-h-screen bg-background">
    <Navigation client:load />
    
    <main class="pt-24 pb-16">
      <div class="container mx-auto px-4 max-w-4xl">
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-foreground mb-6">Why Does Pain Keep Coming Back After Treatment?</h1>
        
        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-muted-foreground">
          <p class="text-xl font-medium text-foreground mb-8 border-l-4 border-primary pl-4">
            When pain keeps returning, it usually means the root cause was never identified — treatment eased the symptom where it hurt without addressing why it started. The reasons differ from person to person: often a movement or stabilisation pattern the body has adapted around, sometimes a nervous system that has grown more sensitive to pain over time. Finding which applies to you takes a <a href="/first-visit" class="text-primary hover:underline">thorough assessment</a>.
          </p>

          <p>You have probably tried a number of things already. Maybe you had some relief — but then, weeks or months later, the same pattern in your lower back, neck, shoulder, or hip shows up again. More often it means the underlying cause hasn't been found and addressed yet.</p>

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-foreground mt-12 mb-4">Why does the same pain keep coming back?</h2>
          <p>The most common reason is the treatment did not adequately address why it hurt. Easing a tight muscle or a painful joint can bring real relief — but if the pattern that overloaded that area in the first place is still there, the pain comes back for a reason.</p>
          <p>This is not unusual. In a large study following people with new low back pain, only about half recovered cleanly; roughly a third relapsed repeatedly over the following two years. Recurring pain is one of the most common patterns in musculoskeletal care.</p>
          <p>What separates lasting relief from the cycle of return is whether the actual cause was found. That is the job of a thorough assessment — and the reasons pain recurs are varied enough that they can only be identified by examining how you move and stabilise, not by assuming.</p>

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-foreground mt-12 mb-4">Two people, identical pain — completely different causes</h2>
          <p>Two people can walk in with the same lower back pain and have entirely different reasons for it. One may have a stabilisation pattern that overloads the spine. Another may have a restricted joint segment feeding the problem from elsewhere. A third may have a long-standing habit — how they sit, lift, or breathe — quietly driving it.</p>
          <p>The pain looks the same. The cause is not. This is why treatment aimed only at the painful spot tends to give temporary relief: it can calm the symptom, but it doesn't reach whichever driver is unique to that person. Without examining how your body moves and stabilises, the same overload keeps happening — and the pain keeps coming back.</p>

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-foreground mt-12 mb-4">I've tried physiotherapy, chiropractic, or massage — why does it still come back?</h2>
          <p>This is one of the most common frustrations patients describe: months of treatment, some relief each time, and then the same problem returns.</p>
          <p>Physiotherapy, chiropractic, and massage therapy are all legitimate, useful tools, and each can help. What determines whether relief lasts is less about which profession you saw and more about whether the underlying cause was found and addressed. Treatment that targets only the painful area — however skilled — leaves the driver in place if that driver sits elsewhere in how you move and stabilise.</p>
          <p>Sciatica is a clear example. Nerve-related leg pain is often driven by patterns in the lower back, pelvis, and hips rather than only at the spot where it's felt. If treatment concentrates on the leg or the local symptom without assessing what is loading the nerve upstream, it's common for the pain to ease and then return. The question that changes the outcome is not "what type of therapist," but "was the actual cause assessed?"</p>

          <h2 id="assessment-finds" class="text-2xl font-bold text-foreground mt-12 mb-4">What a thorough assessment finds that surface treatment misses</h2>
          <p>Two of the most commonly overlooked are worth explaining, because they help make sense of why pain persists.</p>
          <p><strong>A stabilisation pattern the body no longer performs well.</strong> In people with recurrent low back pain, the deep stabilising muscle of the trunk has been shown to activate late, and the movement's control in the brain's motor map reorganises — the nervous system keeps defaulting to an old, compensatory pattern. Importantly, the same research found this is reversible: targeted retraining shifted the motor map back toward normal and restored the muscle's timing.</p>
          <p><strong>A nervous system that has become more sensitive.</strong> When pain persists, the nervous system itself can become more reactive, so that less is needed to trigger it — a well-documented process called central sensitization. This does not mean the pain is imagined; it is a real, physical change in how the system processes signals, and it helps explain why pain can outlast the original injury.</p>
          <p>Neither of these is universal, and neither can be assumed — which is the point. They are found, or ruled out, through the structured history, movement, joint, and muscle testing described on the <a href="/first-visit" class="text-primary hover:underline">Thorough Assessment page</a>.</p>

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-foreground mt-12 mb-4">What actually helps: retraining the pattern</h2>
          <p>Once the assessment identifies what's driving the recurrence, treatment can address it rather than chase the symptom. Where and when a faulty stabilisation or movement pattern is the driver, the aim is to retrain it — because these patterns can be changed with the right, specific practice.</p>
          <p>This is where Dynamic Neuromuscular Stabilization (<a href="#" class="text-primary hover:underline">DNS</a>) fits. DNS is an active approach built on developmental kinesiology — the way our nervous system first learns to stabilise and move as infants. It retrains the deep stabilising system to work automatically again.</p>

          <div class="bg-card p-6 rounded-xl border border-border shadow-sm my-12">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-foreground mb-4">Assessment at Scandinavian Clinic, North Vancouver</h3>
            <p class="mb-6">Eva Andersson trained in Orthopedic Manual Therapy in Sweden and is trained in Dynamic Neuromuscular Stabilization through the Prague School of Rehabilitation. Her focus is on identifying the dysfunctional stabilisation and movement patterns behind recurring pain, and retraining them through specific manual treatment and exercise.</p>
            <a href="#" class="inline-block bg-accent hover:bg-accent/90 text-accent-foreground font-medium py-3 px-6 rounded-md transition-colors">Book a one-on-one assessment →</a>
          </div>

        </div>
      </div>
    </main>
    
    <Footer client:idle />
    <ConsentBanner client:load />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .prose p { margin-bottom: 1.5em; line-height: 1.75; }
</style>
